Z||000011||Formula||11-16-2001||12:04 AM||||Hybrid: I like your wheels!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I think fiberglass is almost the same weight as CF isnt it?  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Untitled-1_copy.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||11-16-2001||12:44 AM||||Fiberglass and carbon fiber do weigh close to the same amount, if you weigh the actual cloth.  The weight savings with carbon fiber are made because you can use much less layering to achieve the same strength.  You see, parts that require a lot of strength will need (for example) 10 layers of fiberglass, where only 2 may be needed with carbon fiber.  This means 8 layers of lost fabric weight, and that much resin is not included.<P>This is why a carbon fiber hood or trunk are kind of a foolish thing to spend money on, in most cases.  The hood is just a skin, doesn't bear any load, so you can use fiberglass and get the same results.  You can even add black dye to fiberglass and get that "black fabric hood" look, just won't have the depth that carbon fiber does up close.  Things that would benefit being made from carbon fiber are things like nose and tail pieces, fender skins, and underbody fairings that get abused in any way.  These parts could be made with much less material, but still wouldn't see a weight gain that would be more than negligible in 99% of cars.<P>I just love carbon fiber because it looks SO DAMN COOL!  Carbon fiber replicates that "deep" look that people pay thousands for on paint jobs, with really nothing different than regular fiberglass except for the fabric.<P>I have started to make some parts that I plan on making fiberglass molds for.  I have considered using carbon fiber for the top (visible) layer, and using fiberglass backing for strength.  This would make some awesome looking panels, especially on my black car.  Not like having a black hood on a white car, for example, so not obnoxious.  You would only see it when you were checking out the car, which would add to the "coolness" factor.  The only way this will work though, is if I have a VERY smooth mold that doesn't show many imperfections, if any.  Otherwise, the surface finish on the carbon fiber won't show off the carbon fiber well, and I'll have wasted 25 bucks a square yard for the carbon fiber.  Well, I guess that's what makes projects so fun though...seeing where they take you <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I guess I kind of got strayed off topic there, but I'm sure nobody will mind too much <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>So, what kind of carbon fiber parts are you guys wanting?  What kind of costs would you like?  I may look at producing something if the demand is there, perhaps within a year, maybe a little more.  I'm sure that you'll have much more luck getting somebody in the Fiero circle to make something than those outside the circle.  Let me know.<P>Oh, by the way, there are carbon fiber dash kits on ebay all the time for 170 bucks (the best rate I've found).  Not sure about aluminum kits, haven't seen anything that caught my eye, and plan on making my own.  I plan on going for a brushed aluminum and carbon fiber theme.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||filthyscarecrow||11-16-2001||01:09 AM||||i'm doing all the bodywork on the FSAE car this year. i was thinkin of makin some pieces out of carbon next summer. my new intake will be carbon fiber. i'll let you know if i come up with anything cool...||||reg||1
Z||000014||artherd||11-16-2001||01:34 AM||||Well, I've been working on both a Hood, and Decklid, as a pet project for some time now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>(one of the many job-hats I wear is occational small-scale carbon parts fab for aerospace industry. Rare that I work on anything over ~12" though, never something as big as a body pannel.)<P>Unfortunately, materials alone are going to be over a thousand dollars for either one. (...)  Then again, I am probally making the parts too strong (too much carbon, not enough 'hollow' core foam.  Won't know for sure untill the part is built and tested.) <P>Perhaps I don't really need to use the freeze pre-epozy cloth either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>I may throw some $ and time at it however if others would be intrested! (<P>So, who would be willing to pay a grand or so for a real carbon hood or decklid?<P>Please direct any intrest via E-mail.<P>Who knows, maybe I'll develop this, then turn it over to a Fiero buisness and make it a product! (Jim?)<P>Hey, what about DOORSKINS, guys?  (they'd be cheaper too...   oooo, you guys have gotten me thinking!)<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000015||cadaver||11-16-2001||09:33 PM||||Well lets see. Where to begin. For real carbon fiber to be done right you dont just simple make the part like you were making a 'glass part.<BR>The resin is an epoxy resin not a polyester or vinylester resin like the stuff bought for doing 'glass.<BR>That is where part of the strength comes from. The cloth is tougher and the resign is tougher.<BR>And now for the the proper procedure. True structual carbon is vacuum bagged and cooked. The vacuuming of it compresses it to be very dense and to remove all the extra resin. Thats what makes it heavy. All the extra resin that is trapped when done by hand and not vacummed. So to do a hood or decklid without the vacuum process it will probablt be heaver than the stock units.<BR>Now there is also a produce called prepreg. It is carbon that is already impregnated with the resign and the initiator. You lay it up and then cook it and that is what kicks it. I have a big roll of carbon. it is thick and stiff. Great for flat or semi flat parts. Foe complex parts you need a looser weave to be able to form the contours eaiser.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Wannabe||11-16-2001||09:49 PM||||A cf decklid and - get this - interior door panels - would be interesting. I have no idea what I'd be willing to pay. It would be good to have an idea what they might cost.||||reg||1
Z||000017||artherd||11-17-2001||01:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B><BR>Now there is also a produce called prepreg. It is carbon that is already impregnated with the resign and the initiator. You lay it up and then cook it and that is what kicks it. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yep, great stuff, you can just compress and heat, no vacumm needed.   That's the thing, extraction the resin after the 'layup' (I almost always use two-sided molds, over-loaded and pressed together.  The expxy removed collapses the works some, and the moulds seal.<P>Makes for a hard, light, and STRONG part.<P>God this stuff is strong.  Forget the body of cars, it could be used in the suspension members. (and is, Ferrari's latest F1 chassis. F300 I belive.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
