Z||000019||Mach10||11-17-2001||02:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Why not get a real wood or brushed aluminum trim kit?  Thats what im gonn eventually do for my car, they're like $200 and have a lot of parts</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hell, I could score a 1/8" slab of aluminum, have it etched, and cut the parts out my self for $100  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ray's right. CF is not much lighter than thin-layered fibreglass. It's only when you get into high-stress bits, and need it chunky that the weight savings start...||||reg||10
Z||000020||TI_3VOM||11-17-2001||04:32 PM||||...here's an idea (and question)...<BR>How about an undercar tray, ala the Ferrari F355? Would it be worth the cost of producing it? Any benefit for our cars to have one? TIA||||reg||5
Z||000021||Nashco||11-18-2001||03:03 AM||||Well, depends.  I think the undercar tray is useless unless you take care of MANY other problems first.  Our cars are lacking in many areas for aerodynamics, so an undercar tray would be like using a bar of gold for a paperweight.<P>I think we've got a lot bigger fish to fry before undercar trays, and would have to be justified with many high speed runs.  Also, to maximize aerodynamics with accuracy requires an aerodynamics wind tunnel..and these are kind of tough to gain access to <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Don't get me wrong, it's a fun idea, just not very feasible or justifiable.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
